===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|chadar|chaddar|chuddar|chadah|chader|chadur|chuddah|chudder|chuddur|chudah}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|hi|चादर}}, from {{der|en|fa-cls|چادر|tr=čādar, čādur}}, from {{der|en|sa|छत्त्र}}.<ref>[http://www.bartleby.com/61/28/C0222800.html Chador in the American Heritage Dictionary of the English Language]</ref><ref name="NED">{{R:Oxford English Dictionary|chuddar|volume=II|page=401|column=1}}</ref> {{doublet|en|chhatra|chatta}}.

Spelling possibly influenced by modern {{m+|fa-ira|چادر|tr=čâdor}} or another language.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[loose]] [[robe]] made from a single [[cloth]], worn as a [[combination]] head [[covering]], [[veil]] and [[shawl]] by chiefly [[Muslim]] [[women]] (and occasionally by those of other faiths) in [[Iran]] and South Asia.
#: {{syn|en|buibui}}
#* {{RQ:Purchas Pilgrimes|part=1|book=1|chapter=The English Ambassadors arrivall at Surat|page=530|line=42|passage=The Commodities are infinite: {{...}} Pintados, Chints and '''Chadors''', Shashes and Girdles, Cannakens{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2023|author=Radhika Iyengar|title=Fire on the Ganges|publisher=Fourth Estate|page=262
|passage=The first few times Dolly stepped out into the city, cloaked by a '''chaddar''' that impaired her vision, she intuitively grabbed Sekond Lal's hand.}}

